BODIES-WAVE TABLE — how it was measured (avra-lane-parallel-main)

1. Engine clock, exact, every family (no perf needed)
   measure.patch (on 323fe0a1; std-program only):
     - QueryTimes.spoken_ms(): every family, own/whole in ms, asks; the core report line uses it.
     - decode tallies (optional): where each answer read back came from (draft/packs/machine/wave/cell)
       and its decode time; printed as "decoded: ..." on each core line.
   Build:   git apply measure.patch && make try          (AVRA_FRAME_POINTERS=1 for step 2)
   Run:     cold; AVRA_QUERY_TIMES=1 AVRA_PROGRAM_NET=0 build/avra-try build packages/cli > log 2>&1
   Table:   python3 families.py log [top-N]
   Note: AVRA_QUERY_TIMES itself costs ~2.6 CPU-s in the bodies wave (QueryTimes.opened/closed).

2. What the CPU does inside the families (perf, frame-pointer build)
   AVRA_FRAME_POINTERS=1 make try (in a separate worktree), then
     perf record -F 250 --call-graph fp -o bw.data -- build/avra-try build packages/cli
     perf script -i bw.data -F pid,tid,time,ip,sym > bw.script
     python3 bwsplit3.py bw.script
   bwsplit3.py: bodies-wave cores = processes whose stacks pass law_findings, the main process left out.
   Each sample goes to its innermost Avra frame: std-program engine code by role (records/frontier,
   memo tables, decode, encode, hash, key fold, store), else the query whose *_compute frame encloses it.

3. Whole-build critical path (busy vs idle per phase)
   sh sample.sh tl <cmd...>          # per-process CPU every 0.25 s
   python3 timeline.py tl 2          # busy cores per 2 s window
   python3 critical.py tl            # serial stretches and waves, idle core-seconds
   Wave waits on claims: each core's "waited Nms: <family> ..." on its core line (AVRA_QUERY_TIMES=1).
